    "application_hypotheses": {
      "02": "LCA Haar is not assumed sigma-finite. The real-line normalization is sigma-finite; compact-uniform continuity gives translation control. Compact metrizability is used for countable density only in that explicit special case.",
      "03": "Nonunital L1 characters use the unitization theorem, not an unital-only assertion. The Fourier algebra is self-adjoint, point separating and nonvanishing, so the C0 approximation theorem applies. Finite complex Radon duality is used in its finite scope. Gaussian signs agree after changing u to minus xi.",
      "09": "Compact cutoffs and compact tails localize finite measures. Haar inversion is used for an abelian group. Fourier approximation on a compact set is bounded by polynomial clipping. Semisimplicity does not identify every measure-algebra character with a Fourier evaluation. Product compactness has no countability restriction.",
      "10": "Quotient averaging uses Cc cutoffs and Cc uniqueness of Radon measures. The measurable integration argument first chooses representatives inside a common open sigma-compact subgroup; its assertion for every representative is explicitly restricted to the sigma-compact case. The p-adic compactness use permits arbitrary products.",
      "11": "Quotient-null pullback is extended across locally determined cosets, not by imposing global sigma-finiteness. Cc density, derivative domination and Gaussian Fourier normalization are used with their required hypotheses. Sampling uses Parseval with inner product linear in the first variable and the stated weights.",
      "17": "Cc density in L2 uses local finiteness and inner regularity on finite-measure sets. The inverse-limit compactness proof uses arbitrary-product compactness. Adelic conjugation/sign identities use abelian Haar inversion."
